1
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:05,080
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:05,780
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:10,140
My name is Kathryn and today we're talking about things at the office.

4
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:11,180
That's right.

5
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:18,360
We are at the office but this time we are going to ask our boss or manager for a raise.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:20,600
Alright, so what is this word raise?

7
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:23,660
Let's take a look at it in today's vocabulary preview.

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:25,910
Vocabulary preview.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,760
Alright, so we're going to ask for a raise.

10
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:35,720
So if you are at your office and you talk to your boss and you say, I want a raise, what does that mean?

11
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:36,990
That means I want more money.

12
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:40,190
Okay, so I want a raise or increase in my salary.

13
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:40,810
That's right.

14
00:00:41,110 --> 00:00:50,510
So a raise is a thing that you ask for and in this case a raise means that you already work at the company, you already have a salary and you want some more money each month.

15
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:53,370
Okay, so that's what we're going to be talking about today.

16
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:54,670
Let's listen to this dialogue.

17
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:03,610
Excuse me sir, may I talk to you?

18
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:05,410
Bill, sure, come on in.

19
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:06,590
What can I do for you?

20
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:12,780
Well sir, as you know, I have been an employee of this prestigious firm for over 10 years.

21
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:13,540
Yes.

22
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,620
I won't beat around the bush.

23
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,200
Sir, I would like a raise.

24
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:22,480
I currently have three companies after me and so I decided to talk to you first.

25
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:24,320
A raise?

26
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:27,870
Son, I would love to give you a raise but this is just not the right time.

27
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:40,530
I understand your position and I know that the current economic downturn has had a negative impact on sales but you must also take into consideration my hard work, proactiveness and loyalty to this company for over a decade.

28
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:50,140
Taking into account these factors and considering I don't want to start a brain drain, I'm willing to offer you a 10% raise and an extra five days of vacation time.

29
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:51,120
How does that sound?

30
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:52,680
Great.

31
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,760
Bill, thank you sir.

32
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,270
Before you go, just out of curiosity, what companies were after you?

33
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,910
Oh, the electric company, the gas company and the water company.

34
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:10,680
Alright, we're back.

35
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:18,320
So now, why don't we take a look at Language Takeaway.

36
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:18,660
Language Takeaway

37
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:26,620
So, the conversation we just heard is a conversation between a manager or a boss and the employee.

38
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:30,500
And the employee says, listen, I need to talk to you about something.

39
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:34,560
I have been an employee at this prestigious firm for over 10 years.

40
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:37,040
So prestigious is our first word.

41
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:38,760
Okay.

42
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:39,960
Prestigious is an adjective, right?

43
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:40,560
That's right.

44
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,680
And it means very respected, very well known.

45
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,080
Esteemed, that's right.

46
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,320
So something that is prestigious has a good reputation.

47
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:50,560
Like for example, some universities.

48
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:58,020
You hear all the time about American universities like Harvard or Yale, which are considered to be prestigious universities.

49
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:02,040
Or the Nobel Peace Prize is a very prestigious award.

50
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:02,740
Okay.

51
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:09,560
So it's an adjective to describe something that's respectable, well known or esteemed, as you said.

52
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:10,560
Exactly.

53
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:11,560
Alright.

54
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,560
And what's our next word?

55
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:13,560
Our next one is not so happy.

56
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:18,540
This is what we've been experiencing in the global economy this last year or two.

57
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:20,800
It's called an economic downturn.

58
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:21,460
Alright.

59
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,660
So an economic downturn is something negative, as you said.

60
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:28,720
So it means that the economy is not doing very well.

61
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:29,280
Right.

62
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,640
So economy, economic, you know what this means.

63
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:37,980
But in this case, downturn is the key part of this phrase to slow down or to decrease.

64
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:44,440
So a downturn is a time when less people are making money and less money overall is being earned.

65
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:45,140
Okay.

66
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:46,640
So an economic downturn.

67
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:47,680
Very good.

68
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:52,720
So let me move on to our next word, which is negative impact.

69
00:03:52,720 --> 00:04:00,580
So the thing working here is the economic downturn and it has had a negative impact on our sales, on this company's sales.

70
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:05,240
So a negative impact, as you know from the first word, is not a good thing.

71
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:05,860
Right.

72
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:07,060
It's an effect, right?

73
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:08,640
That's right.

74
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:09,640
An effect, but that's negative.

75
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:10,480
So it's not a good effect.

76
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:17,840
So I could say that, Marco, being late has had a negative impact on your work lately.

77
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:18,640
Okay.

78
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,640
Or the opposite now.

79
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:22,700
What if something is a good effect on something?

80
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:27,380
So Marco, your hard work has had a really positive impact on this company lately.

81
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:27,960
All right.

82
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:29,680
Good.

83
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,440
So you can use it negative impact or a positive impact.

84
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:31,820
All right.

85
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:33,440
Right.

86
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,760
So impact again is just the effect that something has on something else.

87
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:44,570
And while the boss said that he agrees to give him a raise because in part he didn't want to start a brain drain.

88
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:45,190
What's that?

89
00:04:45,610 --> 00:04:48,310
Oh, this is a very common phrase you hear about countries.

90
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:52,640
They have a brain drain or they want to avoid a brain drain.

91
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:57,090
A drain is the part of a sink or a bathtub where the water goes down.

92
00:04:57,240 --> 00:04:58,240
Okay.

93
00:04:58,240 --> 00:04:59,240
It goes away.

94
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:06,080
And so brain drain means all the brains or intelligent people or educated people leave a country and go to another country.

95
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,550
Or in this case, leave a company and go to another company.

96
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:09,130
All right.

97
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:20,860
So for example, if all the employees at Apple, maybe they start to quit or they start to move jobs and start to work for maybe Microsoft or Google, that's a brain drain, right?

98
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:22,400
That's right.

99
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,000
Because you're talking about a large number of people.

100
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:27,540
Maybe they're all unhappy because they say that working at Apple is not very nice.

101
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,640
So they all leave and go to this other company.

102
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:31,420
The same is true with countries.

103
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:38,840
You can have very, very well-educated people who when they stop studying, they want jobs, they all look somewhere else.

104
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,880
They go to America or Mexico or England.

105
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:44,720
And so that's called a brain drain because they all go away.

106
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:44,920
Okay.

107
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,280
So five great phrases right there and words for you.

108
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:52,040
Let's listen to this dialogue again and we'll be back to take a look at Fluency Builder.

109
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:58,460
Excuse me, sir.

110
00:05:58,880 --> 00:05:59,440
May I talk to you?

111
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:00,880
Bill.

112
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:01,220
Come on in.

113
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:02,400
What can I do for you?

114
00:06:03,070 --> 00:06:08,610
Well, sir, as you know, I have been an employee of this prestigious firm for over 10 years.

115
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:10,720
Yes.

116
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:11,430
I won't beat around the bush.

117
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,350
Sir, I would like a raise.

118
00:06:14,030 --> 00:06:18,330
I currently have three companies after me and so I decided to talk to you first.

119
00:06:19,090 --> 00:06:19,430
A raise?

120
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:23,670
Son, I would love to give you a raise, but this is just not the right time.

121
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:29,490
I understand your position and I know that the current economic downturn has had a negative impact on sales.

122
00:06:30,150 --> 00:06:36,350
But you must also take into consideration my hard work, proactiveness and loyalty to this company for over a decade.

123
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:45,980
Taking into account these factors and considering I don't want to start a brain drain, I'm willing to offer you a 10% raise and an extra five days of vacation time.

124
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:46,940
How does that sound?

125
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:47,560
Great.

126
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:48,360
It's a deal.

127
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:49,340
Thank you, sir.

128
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:54,110
Before you go, just out of curiosity, what companies were after you?

129
00:06:54,570 --> 00:06:59,870
Oh, uh, the electric company, the gas company and the water company.

130
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:01,600
Okay.

131
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:02,920
Okay.

132
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:07,230
This first phrase in today's Fluency Builder is one of my favorites.

133
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:13,470
It's something that we say often when we're having a conversation and we want the other person to just tell us what they're talking about.

134
00:07:13,670 --> 00:07:13,950
All right.

135
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:15,370
So the guy said, you know what?

136
00:07:15,990 --> 00:07:17,250
I'm not going to beat around the bush.

137
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:20,370
Or you could also say, please don't beat around the bush.

138
00:07:20,950 --> 00:07:27,350
So I'm not sure where this phrase comes from, but it means please be direct or I'm going to be direct.

139
00:07:27,790 --> 00:07:31,270
I will not wait any longer to tell you what I'm talking about.

140
00:07:31,410 --> 00:07:35,670
So if somebody is beating around the bush, that means they are not being direct.

141
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:38,200
They're not telling you something immediately.

142
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:48,200
It's you know, like when a person is trying to think, but you know, has a hard time and they're really trying to explain something, but you know, it's just, well, you know what I'm talking about.

143
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,840
It's like that time with that thing where he did that.

144
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:51,380
Okay.

145
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:52,680
This is really annoying.

146
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,960
I know it's called beating around the bush because you just don't say what you're thinking.

147
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:57,960
Right.

148
00:07:57,960 --> 00:07:58,960
Exactly.

149
00:07:58,960 --> 00:07:59,960
So you don't say something directly.

150
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:03,180
Now, our next phrase is to take into consideration.

151
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:05,060
Take something into consideration.

152
00:08:06,030 --> 00:08:09,990
Well this is something that the young man brings up when he's asking for a raise.

153
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:14,620
He says, you should take into consideration all of these great things I do.

154
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:18,150
This means when you make your decision, I want you to think about these things.

155
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:22,320
So consideration means you're thinking when you're trying to make a decision.

156
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:22,750
To consider.

157
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:24,320
All right.

158
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:28,150
So to take something into consideration or you can just say to consider.

159
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:36,750
So I'm interested in buying a new TV, but I have to take into consideration the fact that I don't have a lot of money.

160
00:08:37,490 --> 00:08:39,150
That means I probably won't get a new TV.

161
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:42,160
I have to think about these things when I'm making my decision.

162
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:43,160
Right.

163
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:44,160
Okay.

164
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:45,160
Very good.

165
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:52,880
And our last phrase for today, towards the end, the boss was a little bit interested in knowing what happened or which companies were interested in him.

166
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:54,670
So he said, just out of curiosity.

167
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:59,540
So this is something we say when we don't need to know something.

168
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:08,990
It means that we would like to know because we're curious, we're interested, but not because I need to know right away what companies offer you jobs.

169
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,820
So this is a polite way to ask why someone did something.

170
00:09:12,830 --> 00:09:17,070
So for example, so just out of curiosity, Marco, what kind of shampoo do you use?

171
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:21,840
That is very interesting knowledge that you want there.

172
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:28,170
Well, obviously I don't need to know what kind of shampoo Marco uses, but I'm curious because maybe I'm going to buy a new kind of shampoo.

173
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:31,810
So you can start your sentence by using this phrase, just out of curiosity.

174
00:09:32,610 --> 00:09:34,030
Just out of curiosity.

175
00:09:34,770 --> 00:09:35,410
It's a great one.

176
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:49,040
Listen for that again as we listen to today's dialogue one more time, and we'll be back in a moment to talk about getting a raise.

177
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:49,610
Excuse me, sir.

178
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:50,550
May I talk to you?

179
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:52,370
Sure, come on in.

180
00:09:52,750 --> 00:09:53,530
What can I do for you?

181
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:59,740
Well, sir, as you know, I have been an employee of this prestigious firm for over 10 years.

182
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:01,880
Yes.

183
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,240
I won't beat around the bush, sir.

184
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:05,160
I would like a raise.

185
00:10:05,220 --> 00:10:09,460
I currently have three companies after me, and so I decided to talk to you first.

186
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:11,200
A raise?

187
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:15,440
Son, I would love to give you a raise, but this is just not the right time.

188
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:27,500
I understand your position, and I know that the current economic downturn has had a negative impact on sales, but you must also take into consideration my hard work, proactiveness, and loyalty to this company for over a decade.

189
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:37,070
Taking into account these factors and considering I don't want to start a brain drain, I'm willing to offer you a 10% raise and an extra five days of vacation time.

190
00:10:37,590 --> 00:10:38,030
How does that sound?

191
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:39,680
Great.

192
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:40,680
It's a deal.

193
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,680
Thank you, sir.

194
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:45,210
Before you go, just out of curiosity, what companies were after you?

195
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:50,990
Oh, the electric company, the gas company, and the water company.

196
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:57,400
All right.

197
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:01,260
We're back.

198
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:08,920
So interesting enough, he needed a raise not because three companies wanted to hire him, but because he needed to pay his bills.

199
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:13,460
Well, this is funny because he's not talking about companies, that's right, who are offering him jobs.

200
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:16,800
He's talking about the gas, the water, the electrical company.

201
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:33,920
It's a very funny dialogue, but I think asking for a raise is very hard to do because you know that your boss is worried about money or it's a bad time to ask, but you want to ask because it's important that you get the right compensation for your work.

202
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,900
And actually, it's important to have a clear objective when you want to ask for a raise.

203
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:43,440
So more or less what you want to earn, but plausible within the company.

204
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:45,980
So you're not going to ask for a 100% raise, right?

205
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:51,200
You can maybe aim for a 5 or 10% and most likely you'll get it if you negotiate properly.

206
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:51,920
That's right.

207
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:54,160
And sometimes a raise isn't just about money.

208
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:56,780
You can also, like he did, ask for some time off.

209
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:02,220
So he gets some vacation days or perhaps some training or bonuses or a bonus.

210
00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:08,200
So this all depends on the kind of negotiations that you make, but it's worth it to ask.

211
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:12,320
So please let us know if you've ever had a good time or a bad time asking for a raise.

212
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:15,120
It can be a very nerve wracking experience.

213
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:20,680
I know personally I'm very uncomfortable with it, but we're excited to hear what you have to say.

214
00:12:20,680 --> 00:12:23,360
And if you have any questions or comments, leave them for us on our website, EnglishPod.com.

215
